From 6f37f9840a7d87924fb2827bda4492a76acc1ac3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 21:35:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 86/87] vulkan/wsi/Horizon: a consumer resize re-takes the
 latch it does not hold
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 5 (present modes and scaling)
Why: the layer latch is a one-entry cache keyed on the window that
 asked last, and 0076 made wsi_horizon_layer_moved update it only when
 that key already named this swapchain's window. Two NWindows on one
 physical device are enough to break it: a capabilities query for
 window B re-keys the cache while a swapchain on window A is still
 alive, and the consumer's resize report that then reaches A is
 discarded — `moved` is false, the log line is not printed and the
 latch is not written. The next capabilities query for A relatches
 through nwindowGetDimensions, which prefers the producer's
 NWindow::width — A's own registration — over the output the consumer
 reported, so currentExtent and maxImageExtent go on describing a size
 the output no longer has. That is precisely the stale pair 0070
 exists to prevent, arrived at from a third direction.
Evidence: wsi_horizon.c as 0076 left it:
   const bool moved = wsi->layer_window == chain->window &&
                      (wsi->layer.width != reported.width ||
                       wsi->layer.height != reported.height);
 and, forty lines up, struct wsi_horizon: "A second window on the same
 physical device re-takes it rather than inheriting the first one's,
 which is the only thing a one-entry cache has to get right" —
 wsi_horizon_layer_extent re-takes it exactly that way, and this
 function did not.
 Class X: cross-built 2026-09-08 with this tree's flags,
 aarch64-none-elf-gcc (devkitA64) 15.2.0, no warning. Unreachable on
 the console so far for two separate reasons: dock/mode_change measured
 on 2026-09-07 that the layer does not move when the display mode does,
 and nothing in the tests opens a second NWindow.
Upstream: no — the backend is Horizon's.

`reported` has already been isolated as the consumer's own voice by
wsi_horizon_extent_changed, so it is the better value whatever the
cache happens to hold. Both fields are written, which is what re-taking
a one-entry cache means.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 136c122..83ea967 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -1730,11 +1730,31 @@ wsi_horizon_layer_moved(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
 
    simple_mtx_lock(&wsi->lock);
 
-   const bool moved = wsi->layer_window == chain->window &&
-                      (wsi->layer.width != reported.width ||
-                       wsi->layer.height != reported.height);
-   if (moved)
+   /* THE CACHE IS RE-TAKEN, NOT CONSULTED FOR PERMISSION.
+    *
+    * `layer_window` names whichever window asked last, because that is
+    * all a one-entry cache can hold (see struct wsi_horizon). A second
+    * NWindow on this physical device therefore evicts the first one's
+    * latch merely by having its capabilities queried — and if this
+    * function then insisted on finding its own window there, the
+    * consumer's resize of THIS window would be dropped on the floor.
+    * The next query for it would relatch through nwindowGetDimensions,
+    * which prefers the producer's NWindow::width — this swapchain's
+    * own registration — over the output the consumer just reported,
+    * and currentExtent and maxImageExtent would still be the stale
+    * pair 0070 exists to prevent.
+    *
+    * `reported` has already been isolated as the consumer's voice by
+    * wsi_horizon_extent_changed, so it is the better value whatever
+    * the cache currently holds. Both fields are written. */
+   const bool retaken = wsi->layer_window != chain->window;
+   const bool moved = retaken ||
+                      wsi->layer.width != reported.width ||
+                      wsi->layer.height != reported.height;
+   if (moved) {
+      wsi->layer_window = chain->window;
       wsi->layer = reported;
+   }
 
    simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
 
